WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:07.701
It is great to welcome today Dr. Bandy X lead
to the program, who is a Yale University forensic

00:00:07.701 --> 00:00:09.400
psychiatrist.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:14.940
And this is maybe a field, Dr. Lee, that not
everybody is familiar with.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:19.079
Can you talk a little bit about forensic psychiatry
and the work that you do?

00:00:19.079 --> 00:00:20.160
Yes.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.939
Forensic psychiatrist works at the interface
of psychiatry and the law.

00:00:24.939 --> 00:00:31.829
And so in terms of my clinical work, I mainly
work in prisons or state hospitals where many

00:00:31.829 --> 00:00:35.850
legal issues may become important.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:45.120
And we do evaluations for courts such as capacity
to stand trial or criminal responsibility.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:50.100
I do civil cases such as asylum cases.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:56.140
And I also have been teaching at Yale Law
School for 15 years.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:00.260
And so there's a lot of overlap between the
law and psychiatry.

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:09.540
So one of the the general skills and abilities
seems to be evaluating sort of the state,

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:15.570
the emotional state, the cognitive state of
individuals, often in the context of criminal

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:18.530
proceedings, but also in other contexts as
well.

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:19.690
Yes.

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:26.680
So you have grown quite a following on Twitter
and elsewhere because of some of the observations

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:32.330
you've made about the president, Donald Trump,
and and to to make it totally transparent

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:33.480
to the audience.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:38.770
You're very concerned about the behavior and
the actions of the president.

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:43.810
When when did this first become something
you started paying attention to and started

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:44.920
to speak out about it?

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.409
What was it that you were seeing from the
president?

00:01:47.409 --> 00:01:56.579
You know, I was very distant from any of the
political world and was mainly working as

00:01:56.579 --> 00:02:00.310
a clinician and academic.

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:05.579
And although I have consulted with a number
of governments on public policy and prison

00:02:05.579 --> 00:02:13.290
reform in particular, I had not considered
politics to be a domain for me to speak about.

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:20.290
But the day after Donald Trump's presidential
election, I was contacted by numerous members

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:27.010
from the public, from civil society members
and others who had known of my work.

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:31.540
And so I was inundated with phone calls and
messages.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:38.409
And I had to think to myself, if I have been
working as a violence expert, which is my

00:02:38.409 --> 00:02:47.040
area within forensic psychiatry and also in
public health, I had been working to prevent

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:49.400
violence at multiple levels.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:51.890
Individual, collective, societal.

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:59.170
And would I turn away from this situation
when it poses perhaps the greatest level of

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:06.110
danger that we could ever face and potentially
be a trigger for the greatest amount of violence

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:07.349
in society?

00:03:07.349 --> 00:03:11.640
What were the first behaviors of Donald Trump's
that that made you realize that there's that

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:15.170
possibility of danger and violence?

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:23.060
Well, there are many trigger signs that we
look for in terms of danger and violence proneness

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:32.250
and and because past violence is often an
indicator for potential future violence.

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:38.299
We look at things like verbal aggression,
history of boasting about sexual assaults

00:03:38.299 --> 00:03:48.400
and inciting violence, as he has done at his
rallies and definitely during the presidency.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:54.710
He has taunted allies and enemy nations with
nuclear power.

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:57.640
And all these are signs of danger.

00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:03.170
Of course, there are also psychological characteristics
that he exhibited, including impulsivity,

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:11.299
recklessness, a need to constantly burnish
a sense of power, preoccupation with a grandiose

00:04:11.299 --> 00:04:12.900
sense of self image.

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:18.850
All of these things are associated with violence
and therefore things to look out for.

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:22.880
So inevitably, and even in the responses that
you sometimes get on Twitter, people will

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:25.230
bring up the Goldwater rule, the Goldwater
rule.

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:26.330
You're not supposed to diagnose.

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:32.380
You're not supposed to assign conditions to
people who you have not examined in person.

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:36.950
And increasingly, I'm reading more and more
about how that was really a sort of political

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:46.160
convention more than any kind of some sort
of definitive clinical consideration, and

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:50.780
that it dates back to Barry Goldwater and
the idea that there was an effort made to

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:56.480
prevent so-called armchair diagnosis of what
of what we're concerns about, about Goldwater.

00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:01.910
Before we go further, maybe you should address
that in terms of what what is OK for you to

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:05.840
observe and comment on having not examined
Donald Trump in person?

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:12.890
Well, first of all, the Goldwater rule is
unusual in that it was, as you say, a political

00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:19.080
compromise that was not it did not derive
out of research or.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:30.190
Clinical concerns or professional practice,
but rather out of a lawsuit against popular

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:36.750
newspaper at the time called Fact Magazine,
which had done a survey of psychiatrists,

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:44.370
and it was unusual for the American Psychiatric
Association to come up with an ethical, so-called

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:48.710
ethical guideline in response to political
situation.

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:52.990
But under the Trump presidency, it has certainly
been abused.

00:05:52.990 --> 00:06:01.070
It has also been modified about a couple of
weeks after a couple months after Donald Trump's

00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:02.960
inauguration.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:08.030
They modified it to cover not just diagnosis,
but any comment on a public figure of any

00:06:08.030 --> 00:06:13.120
kind that actually goes against the original
Goldwater rule because the original Goldwater

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:20.300
rule was an affirmative obligation and that
we should participate in activities that promote

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:21.560
public health.

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:28.120
And so when we're asked about a public figure,
we should educate the public to improve its

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:33.080
health and well-being, just not diagnose,
because the diagnosis you cannot do unless

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:34.520
you have all the medical records.

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:41.500
And nowadays we do diagnosis not by personal
interview alone, but by external observation

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:47.860
and getting collateral information from those
who have directly interacted with the person.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:48.860
So.

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:54.930
So one might say that the rule has also been
invalid since 1980.

00:06:54.930 --> 00:07:01.570
Definitively, it was considered an outdated
since its inception in 1973.

00:07:01.570 --> 00:07:07.600
But since 1980, our diagnostic practices have
changed that we don't just rely on a personal

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:09.090
interview.

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:11.330
So that having been clarified.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:15.010
I'd love to get your perspective on that.

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:20.640
Where or if sort of some different areas of
concern with the president overlap or where

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:21.640
they are separate.

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:26.510
So you've already talked about the sort of
personality traits past glorification of of

00:07:26.510 --> 00:07:28.310
alleged sexual assaults, etc.

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:34.360
You've kind of outlined that we then have
his assertions about the cognitive test he

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:39.280
has taken and claims to have passed that we
believe, is the Montreal cognitive assessment,

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:44.840
which includes identifying a few animals counting
backwards from 100 by seven, drawing a clock,

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:46.340
remembering words, etc..

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:47.340
There's that piece.

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:52.420
And I'm curious if and how that interacts
with your observations so far.

00:07:52.420 --> 00:08:01.190
And then there is also these the mispronunciation
of words yesterday, in fact, repeatedly referring

00:08:01.190 --> 00:08:05.900
to fatalities as fatalities and having an
expression on his face that he seems to know

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:08.880
he's saying something wrong, but unable to
correct it.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:14.580
The orange is of an investigation is another
example where there was an expression of recognition

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:19.110
that something was wrong, but unable to say,
oh, no, I mean, the origins of an investigation.

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:21.560
Are these three categories related?

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:23.150
Are they separate?

00:08:23.150 --> 00:08:25.750
How do they relate?

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:28.060
If so?

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:29.220
So.

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:37.110
I had been listening to Donald Trump's interviews
about this recent so-called recent cognitive

00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:39.040
tests that he's taken.

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:44.630
And it turns out it is the test that he took
over two and a half years ago under White

00:08:44.630 --> 00:08:47.080
House Doctor Ronnie Jackson.

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:48.080
Yes.

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:53.700
And and you're correct in that it turns out
to be the Montreal cognitive assessment, which

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:55.300
really is not.

00:08:55.300 --> 00:09:01.930
It's first of all, not a fitness exam, as
Dr. Jackson tried to assert it was.

00:09:01.930 --> 00:09:10.570
And also, it is not even a good cognitive
screening because research studies have shown

00:09:10.570 --> 00:09:18.220
that full blown Alzheimer patients and hospitalized
schizophrenia patients can equally ace the

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:19.220
test.

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:24.541
And so, therefore, the Alzheimer's Research
Consortium came out stating that the screen

00:09:24.541 --> 00:09:30.900
should not replace a full neuropsychiatric
or neuropsychological examination when it's

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:31.900
indicated.

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:38.840
Well, for the president, it certainly is indicated
because he has shown not only numerous, multiple

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:45.580
very grave signs of cognitive impairment as
well as decline over time.

00:09:45.580 --> 00:09:53.340
We have considerable information on him because
of multiple public appearances over time.

00:09:53.340 --> 00:10:02.110
And it shows a pattern of decline that is
recognizable for clinicians, in other words.

00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:07.570
Often people become concerned about Joe Biden,
for example.

00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:17.210
But in comparison, Mr. Biden's impairments
are superficial and more recognizable as non

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:27.880
concerning ones, whereas Mr. Trump's impairments
are fit the pattern of very serious ongoing

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:33.570
cognitive decline that is related to his own
family history of dementia, which he would

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:37.340
be predisposed to because of this family history.

00:10:37.340 --> 00:10:42.180
And now I'm not really making a diagnosis
here because I'd like to point out that this

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:48.950
can indicate multiple possibilities of cognitive
decline.

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:56.330
For one does not simply come about because
of dementia or other neurodegenerative diseases

00:10:56.330 --> 00:11:05.710
that can occur because of stress, because
of worsening psychiatric conditions that he

00:11:05.710 --> 00:11:12.020
may otherwise have, or it can be a whole host
of other things.

00:11:12.020 --> 00:11:16.790
So that's why we don't diagnose without having
all the medical records and without doing

00:11:16.790 --> 00:11:21.110
a fitting examination, even if it doesn't
include a personal interview.

00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:27.200
We need to do an examination of the data that
are that are appropriate.

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:34.850
And so what it does indicate, however, is
that we are seeing signs of very serious pathological

00:11:34.850 --> 00:11:40.690
processes that need a thorough examination,
not a 10 minute screen like Montreal cognitive

00:11:40.690 --> 00:11:41.690
assessment.

00:11:41.690 --> 00:11:49.390
And I believe that the president has been
bringing this up himself because he himself

00:11:49.390 --> 00:11:52.930
is concerned about his own mental fitness.

00:11:52.930 --> 00:12:00.330
He often brings up topics when he feels that
he needs to fight his own notions.

00:12:00.330 --> 00:12:05.790
And, of course, others as well, that he may
not be fit for duty.

00:12:05.790 --> 00:12:08.660
And he has very valid reasons for concern.

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:15.410
But he does this by trying to convince himself
that he that he is that.

00:12:15.410 --> 00:12:19.290
And in addition to trying to convince others.

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:24.930
And so he is repeatedly bringing up this test
that he had done two and a half years ago,

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:33.310
I think relates to Dr. Ronnie Jackson, who
was a White House employed and as a military

00:12:33.310 --> 00:12:35.550
officer, subordinate to the commander in chief.

00:12:35.550 --> 00:12:39.510
And so he is he would not be in a position
to do a fitness test.

00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:46.670
He does not have the independence, but he
still use this ten minute screen to declare

00:12:46.670 --> 00:12:50.440
the president mentally fit in a press conference.

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:55.350
And so that in itself is is a considerable
breach of medical ethics.

00:12:55.350 --> 00:13:07.640
I don't know whether or not he was aware of
his false declarations, but because of this

00:13:07.640 --> 00:13:14.279
episode of his perhaps admin or administering
a test that really would not tell us very

00:13:14.279 --> 00:13:15.279
much.

00:13:15.279 --> 00:13:20.170
But doing so anyhow was perhaps an attempt
to reassure the president.

00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:27.760
But above all, it ended up misleading the
public when when the public was raising many

00:13:27.760 --> 00:13:29.080
questions about the.

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:34.910
President's fitness now Dr. Jackson has been
removed from his position about two years

00:13:34.910 --> 00:13:36.100
ago.

00:13:36.100 --> 00:13:43.000
And amid other allegations that would make
his medical ethics and standards of practice

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:44.860
questionable.

00:13:44.860 --> 00:13:46.610
We're going to go to a break on the podcast.

00:13:46.610 --> 00:13:47.610
TV and radio show.

00:13:47.610 --> 00:17:09.500
But we'll have our complete conversation with
Dr. Leon on our YouTube channel.

00:17:09.500 --> 00:18:41.690
The David Pakman Show.

00:18:41.690 --> 00:25:25.480
David Pakman dadgum.

